<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Before you start</h2>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Chrome or Edge, on a computer or on Android</li>
    <li>The device on the ground, with a GPS fix</li>
    <li>A battery above the warning, or charging</li>
    <li>The release file ending in .signed.bin</li>
    <li>No other app holding the device&rsquo;s settings</li>
    <li>Ten minutes, with the device close to you</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">What happens</h2>

  <h3>One question on the glass, <span class="highlight">then it installs itself</span>.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Install asks the device to take the image you chose. The glass says
    <span class="mono">FIRMWARE</span>, then
    <span class="mono">INSTALL</span> and the version, for example
    <span class="mono">INSTALL 0.2.0+15 FROM THE PHONE</span>: press twice. That
    opens a window of ten minutes, and only while the device knows it is on the
    ground.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The image travels next. The glass says <span class="mono">RECEIVING</span>
    and the page shows how much of the image has arrived. One press on the
    device stops it. The window also closes at ten minutes, at a take-off, or
    when the link drops. Install again and the upload picks up where it
    stopped, as long as the device has not restarted in between.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Once the last byte is in, the device checks that it holds the version it
    asked you about, that it is still on the ground and that the battery can
    finish the job, then installs without asking again. It paints
    <span class="mono">INSTALLING</span> and restarts into the new firmware,
    which takes about thirty seconds, and the link drops.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Connect again. The new firmware starts on probation: it has to bring its
    radio up, hear its GPS receiver and draw its screen before it keeps itself.
    If it does not, the device goes back to the firmware it had, and this page
    says so rather than leave you looking at an old version number.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Settings</h2>

  <h3>What it holds, <span class="highlight">beside what it ships on</span>.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Once connected, the page reads the aircraft type, the callsign, the units
    and the alarm from the device, and asks the firmware for the values a new
    unit ships on. A setting that differs from those says so under its field.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Save sends only what you changed. Back to defaults sends the firmware&rsquo;s
    own values for what differs. Either way the glass says
    <span class="mono">SETTINGS</span>,
    <span class="mono">APPLY THE SETTINGS THE PHONE SENT</span>: press twice,
    and the page reads the settings back from the device.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The radar&rsquo;s range and plot are set on the device&rsquo;s own menu,
    and the page leaves them as they are.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>The page says</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Saved</th><td data-manage-word="saved">The device stored the settings, and the form shows them as the device read them back.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">What it tells you about itself</h2>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>The device reports</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th class="mono">probation</th><td data-manage-word="probation">The new firmware is proving itself: radio up, receiver talking, screen drawn. This resolves on its own within seconds, there is nothing to do.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">reverted</th><td data-manage-word="reverted">The last update started but did not prove itself, so the device went back to the firmware it had. Nothing is broken: the version number is the old one on purpose. Try again, and if it happens twice, tell us which version you tried.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">refused</th><td data-manage-word="image_refused">The bootloader would not start the last update, and the device kept the firmware it had: the image did not pass its signature check. Download it again, and if it happens twice, tell us which file you used.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">settings: prior</th><td data-manage-word="settings_prior">Your settings are the ones from before that update: what you changed while the newer firmware ran stayed with it.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">settings: defaults</th><td data-manage-word="settings_defaults">The stored settings could not be read, so the device runs on its defaults. Check the callsign and the aircraft type before you fly.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">swap_powered: false</th><td data-manage-word="swap_unpowered">The battery is critical, too low to install. Charge it first.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">went_dark_flat</th><td data-manage-word="went_dark_flat">The last time it switched off, it was for a flat battery. Charge it before you update.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">When it says no</h2>

  <p class="lede">
    Every refusal comes with a reason, and the page puts it in the words below.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>The device answers</th><th>What it means, and what to do</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th class="mono">in_flight</th><td data-manage-word="in_flight">It has not proven it is on the ground: it is flying, or has had no GPS fix since it was switched on. Take it where it sees the sky, wait for the fix, then try again.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">low_power</th><td data-manage-word="low_power">The battery is critical. Installing is 15 to 25 seconds of flash erases, and the device does not start one on a cell that might not finish it. Charge it, then try again.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">expired</th><td data-manage-word="expired">Nobody answered the question on the glass within thirty seconds, which counts as a refusal. Try again, and press twice when the glass asks.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">cancelled</th><td data-manage-word="cancelled">The device said no: one press refuses the question, and one press on RECEIVING stops the upload.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">nothing_staged</th><td data-manage-word="nothing_staged">The upload ended, but the device could not read the image back. Install again to send it.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">not_approved</th><td data-manage-word="not_approved">The image that arrived is not the version the glass asked you about, so the device did not install it. Install again.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">claimed</th><td data-manage-word="claimed">Another app holds this device&rsquo;s settings. Disconnect it or close it, then connect again.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">window_closed</th><td data-manage-word="window_closed">The upload window closed: it lasts ten minutes, and a take-off, one press on the glass or a dropped link closes it sooner. Install again: the upload picks up where it stopped, unless the device restarted.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">older</th><td data-manage-word="older">This image is older than the firmware on the device, and a device on released firmware never goes back over Bluetooth. The same version installs again, a newer one too.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">wrong_key</th><td data-manage-word="wrong_key">This image is not signed with the key the device trusts: a development build for a released device, or the other way round. Choose an image of the device&rsquo;s kind, or change kinds with a .uf2 on the USB drive.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">too_large</th><td data-manage-word="too_large">This image is bigger than the room the device keeps for an update, so it is not a skyBlip Go image.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">not_image</th><td data-manage-word="not_image">This file is not a signed firmware image. Choose the one ending in .signed.bin: the .uf2 is for the USB drive.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">flash</th><td data-manage-word="flash">The device could not write the image to its memory. Try again, and if it happens again, use the USB drive.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">unknown_cmd</th><td data-manage-word="unknown_cmd">The firmware on the device is older than this page and does not know the question. Update it with the USB drive.</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="mono">anything else</th><td data-manage-word="refused">The device refused, with a reason this page does not know:</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

